            {
              "id": "0ed5fd0d-2c74-3c4c-b277-189b95162db1",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have protection against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. In a CSRF attack, an attacker tricks a victim into performing actions on their behalf on a web application in which the victim is authenticated.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the POST route '/modifyproduct' lacks CSRF protection. This means that if an authenticated user is tricked into clicking a malicious link or loading a malicious site, the attacker could potentially modify product details on behalf of the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement CSRF tokens in your application. CSRF tokens are unique, random values associated with a user's session, which are required to be sent with every state-changing request. This ensures that only the legitimate user can perform actions in their session, as only they have access to their CSRF token.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `csurf` middleware in Express.js to implement CSRF protection. Here's how you can modify the provided code to include CSRF protection:\n\n```javascript\nvar csurf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csurf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/modifyproduct', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.modifyProductSubmit);\n```\n\nIn this code, `csurf` middleware is added to the '/modifyproduct' POST route. This middleware generates a CSRF token and associates it with the user's session. It also validates the token sent with every request to this route.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- csurf: CSRF protection middleware for Express.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "194ddd46-e637-3aac-9bd6-f279545ed3bf",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.sequelize.query()` is not properly handled in case of a rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include error handling for promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` method to the promise, which will be invoked when the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` method should contain logic to handle the error appropriately, such as logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.sequelize.query(query, {\n\tmodel: db.User\n}).then(user => {\n\tif (user.length) {\n\t\tvar output = {\n\t\t\tuser: {\n\t\t\t\tname: user[0].name,\n\t\t\t\tid: user[0].id\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.render('app/usersearch', {\n\t\t\toutput: output\n\t\t})\n\t} else {\n\t\treq.flash('warning', 'User not found')\n\t\tres.render('app/usersearch', {\n\t\t\toutput: null\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred while processing your request.');\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, if the promise returned by `db.sequelize.query()` is rejected, the `.catch()` method will be invoked. The error will be logged to the console and a response with a status code of 500 (Internal Server Error) will be sent to the client.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "19d2a2dc-74e6-3047-98f6-987c4094ba29",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.findAll()` is not being handled properly. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem connecting to the database), there is no `.catch()` block to handle the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always include error handling for promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be executed if the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should handle the error in a way that does not disrupt the application or expose sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n\twhere: {\n\t\tname: {\n\t\t\t[Op.like]: '%' + req.body.name + '%'\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(products => {\n\toutput = {\n\t\tproducts: products,\n\t\tsearchTerm: req.body.name\n\t}\n\tres.render('app/products', {\n\t\toutput: output\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle the error here. For example, you might log the error and render an error page.\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).render('error', { error: err });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.Product.findAll()`)\n- Express (for `req` and `res`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1b1b1c48-c47c-359a-9cdc-094d5ac6f7db",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the query is returning all fields of the product with the specified id, which may include fields that should not be exposed to the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n    where: {\n        'id': req.query.id\n    },\n    attributes: ['id', 'name', 'description'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(product => {\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `find` method will only return the `id`, `name`, and `description` fields of the product.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1fae96e7-4e84-34c7-bfea-ce5a023516f1",
              "name": "Insecure Logging Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization is a vulnerability that occurs when the Sequelize initialization does not properly sanitize or escape SQL queries before logging them. This can lead to sensitive information being exposed in the logs, such as database credentials, table structures, or even the data itself. This vulnerability can be exploited by an attacker to gain unauthorized access to the database or perform other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable SQL logging in production environments. If logging is necessary, ensure that sensitive information is properly sanitized or redacted before being logged. Additionally, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nvar sequelize = new Sequelize(process.env.DATABASE_URL, {\n  logging: false\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, the `logging` option is set to `false` to disable SQL logging.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: This is an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library for Node.js. It provides a high-level API for interacting with SQL databases.\n- `dotenv`: This is a zero-dependency module that loads environment variables from a `.env` file into `process.env`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2210a9d6-f3a3-3d11-9eaa-757cf47b15cd",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `db.User.findAll({})` is not being handled for a case where it might get rejected.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using `try/catch` with async/await. This allows you to handle any errors that occur during the execution of the Promise and prevent unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findAll({})\n  .then(users => {\n    res.status(200).json({\n      success: true,\n      users: users\n    });\n  })\n  .catch(err => {\n    console.error(err);\n    res.status(500).json({\n      success: false,\n      message: 'An error occurred while fetching users'\n    });\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, if the Promise returned by `db.User.findAll({})` is rejected, the `.catch()` block will handle the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Sequelize (for `db.User.findAll({})`) and Express (for `res.status().json()`). So, the library dependencies required by the code example to execute properly are:\n\n- Sequelize\n- Express\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP - Error Handling](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Error_Handling_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24e62fbb-cc51-3bda-9264-8b50b8e25682",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure Direct Object References (IDOR) vulnerability occurs when an application provides direct access to objects based on user-supplied input. In this case, the application uses user input directly to query its data without any additional validation or authorization checks. This can allow an attacker to bypass authorization and directly access data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is using the user-supplied input (`req.body.id`) directly to query the database for a user. This can lead to IDOR vulnerability if the `id` is predictable or guessable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Ensure that the user-supplied input is valid and expected. For example, you can check if the `id` is a number and within the expected range.\n\n2. Implement proper access controls: Ensure that the user is authorized to access the requested data. For example, you can check if the user is the owner of the requested user data.\n\n3. Use indirect object references: Instead of using direct references to database records, use indirect references that are mapped to the user's session.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\n// Validate user input\nif (!Number.isInteger(req.body.id)) {\n  throw new Error('Invalid input');\n}\n\n// Check user authorization\nif (req.user.id !== req.body.id) {\n  throw new Error('Unauthorized');\n}\n\n// Query the database\ndb.User.find({\n  where: {\n    'id': req.body.id\n  }\n}).then(user => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, used to handle HTTP requests and responses.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "25522f94-f42e-30ca-be00-6ae69db87632",
              "name": "Assigning To InnerHTML Can Introduce DOM XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential DOM XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is assigned to `innerHTML` in JavaScript. This is because `innerHTML` interprets any HTML that it is given, including `<script>` tags, which can be used to execute malicious scripts. In the provided code snippet, `users[i].id` is assigned to `innerHTML`, which could be a potential security risk if `users[i].id` contains user-controlled data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `innerHTML` to directly insert user-controlled data into your HTML. Instead, consider using `textContent` or `innerText`, which do not interpret the assigned values as HTML. Alternatively, you can sanitize the user-controlled data before assigning it to `innerHTML`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.innerHTML = users[i].id;\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.textContent = users[i].id;\n```\n\nOr, if you need to insert HTML, sanitize the user-controlled data first:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.innerHTML = sanitize(users[i].id);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2e4a519a-8118-31d1-aace-e3fb22ddc603",
              "name": "Assigning To InnerHTML Can Introduce DOM XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential DOM XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is assigned to `innerHTML` in JavaScript. This is because `innerHTML` interprets any HTML that it is given, which includes potentially malicious scripts. If an attacker can control the input that is assigned to `innerHTML`, they can inject a script that will be executed by the browser.\n\nIn the provided code example, the user's email is directly assigned to `innerHTML`. If the email is not properly sanitized and contains a script, it will be executed when the email is displayed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `innerHTML` with user-controlled data. Instead, use `textContent` or `innerText`, which do not interpret HTML and therefore prevent script execution. \n\nIf you must use `innerHTML`, ensure that the user-controlled data is properly sanitized to remove any potential scripts. This can be done using a library that provides HTML sanitization, such as DOMPurify.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code with one of the following, depending on your needs:\n\n```javascript\n// Option 1: Use textContent\nc_email.textContent = users[i].email;\n\n// Option 2: Use innerText\nc_email.innerText = users[i].email;\n\n// Option 3: Use DOMPurify to sanitize the email before assigning it to innerHTML\nc_email.innerHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(users[i].email);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly. However, if you choose to use DOMPurify for sanitization, you will need to include it in your project.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "304c736c-8339-3edd-8a47-866f035a5f6c",
              "name": "Predictable Token Using Weak Hashing",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a security flaw where an attacker can manipulate the input to the MD5 hash function, potentially leading to unauthorized access or other security breaches. In the provided code snippet, the application is comparing a user-provided token (req.query.token) with the MD5 hash of another user-provided input (req.query.login). If an attacker can predict or control the 'login' input, they can potentially generate a matching 'token' and bypass the security check.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid using MD5 for security-sensitive operations**: MD5 is a weak hash function with known vulnerabilities. For security-sensitive operations like password storage or token generation, use a stronger hash function like SHA-256 or bcrypt.\n\n2. **Avoid directly comparing user-controlled inputs for authentication**: Instead of directly comparing user-provided inputs for authentication, use a secure method like password-based key derivation functions (PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt).\n\n3. **Use a secure method for token generation**: Instead of generating tokens based on user-provided input, use a secure, random method for token generation. This reduces the risk of an attacker being able to predict or control the token.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\n// Generate a secure, random token\nfunction generateToken() {\n    return crypto.randomBytes(64).toString('hex');\n}\n\n// Store the token securely\nlet token = generateToken();\nlet hashedToken = bcrypt.hashSync(token, 10);\n\n// When checking the token, use bcrypt's compare function\nif (bcrypt.compareSync(req.query.token, hashedToken)) {\n    // The tokens match\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- bcrypt\n- crypto\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP - Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-328: Reversible One-Way Hash](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-328",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-328"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "36ab68b6-b208-391a-a5a6-c2dfbf99a3a3",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.find()` is not handled properly. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a database error), there is no `.catch()` block to handle the error, which can lead to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises. This allows you to define how your application should behave in the event of a rejection, such as logging the error or returning a user-friendly error message.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t}\n}).then(product => {\n\tif (!product) {\n\t\tproduct = new db.Product()\n\t}\n\tproduct.code = req.body.code\n\tproduct.name = req.body.name\n\tproduct.description = req.body.description\n\tproduct.tags = req.body.tags\n\tproduct.save().then(p => {\n\t\tif (p) {\n\t\t\treq.flash('success', 'Product added/modified!')\n\t\t\tres.redirect('/app/products')\n\t\t}\n\t}).catch(err => {\n\t\toutput = {\n\t\t\tproduct: product\n\t\t}\n\t\treq.flash('danger',err)\n\t\tres.render('app/modifyproduct', {\n\t\t\toutput: output\n\t\t})\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\t// Handle the error appropriately here\n});\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- A database library (such as Sequelize) to provide the `db.Product.find()` and `product.save()` methods.\n- An Express.js-like library to provide the `req` and `res` objects and methods.\n- A flash messaging library (such as connect-flash) to provide the `req.flash()` method.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "36d8653a-5a9f-3edf-8915-bd143d78b5a8",
              "name": "Dangerous Code Evaluation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Code Injection Vulnerability Detected\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an attacker can inject and execute malicious code within your application. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when user input is processed unsafely. In the provided code example, the `mathjs.eval()` function is used to evaluate a string from the request body as a mathematical expression. If an attacker can control the content of `req.body.eqn`, they can potentially execute arbitrary JavaScript code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `eval()` or similar functions that execute strings as code whenever possible. If you must use such a function, ensure that user input is properly sanitized and validated before it is processed. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `mathjs.eval()`, consider using `mathjs.parse()` and `mathjs.compile()`, which can safely evaluate mathematical expressions without executing arbitrary code. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nconst math = require('mathjs');\nconst expr = math.parse(req.body.eqn);\nconst code = expr.compile();\nconst scope = {};\nconst result = code.evaluate(scope);\noutput = result;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `mathjs`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3bbf8bd3-8591-33d4-9a09-c69b97cb5635",
              "name": "Insecure Serialization Library Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `node-serialize` library in JavaScript is vulnerable to arbitrary code execution. This vulnerability allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the server by providing a serialized object with a malicious payload. This is possible because the `node-serialize` library uses the `eval()` function to deserialize objects, which can execute any JavaScript code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using the `node-serialize` library to deserialize untrusted data. If you must use this library, make sure to validate and sanitize the input before deserializing it. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `node-serialize`, consider using a safer alternative like `JSON.parse()` and `JSON.stringify()`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nvar obj = {foo: 'bar'};\nvar serializedObj = JSON.stringify(obj);\nvar deserializedObj = JSON.parse(serializedObj);\n```\n\nIn this example, `JSON.stringify()` is used to serialize the object, and `JSON.parse()` is used to deserialize it. This is safer because `JSON.parse()` does not execute the JavaScript code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `node-serialize` library.\n\n```javascript\nvar serialize = require(\"node-serialize\");\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3d0bed17-db26-3418-a199-b5eb7b43788b",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet above is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. In this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that user-controlled data (`req.body.name`) is directly used in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts, which will be executed in the browser of any user viewing the rendered page.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user-controlled data before including it in an HTML string. This can be done using a variety of methods, including HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, CSS encoding, and URL encoding, depending on the context in which the data is used.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `escape-html` library to escape user-controlled data:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\ndb.Product.findAll({\n\twhere: {\n\t\tname: {\n\t\t\t[Op.like]: '%' + escapeHtml(req.body.name) + '%'\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(products => {\n\toutput = {\n\t\tproducts: products,\n\t\tsearchTerm: escapeHtml(req.body.name)\n\t}\n\tres.render('app/products', {\n\t\toutput: output\n\t})\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.Product.findAll` and `Op.like`)\n- Express.js (for `req.body.name` and `res.render`)\n- escape-html (for `escapeHtml`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "409c0470-0d6d-3030-ad5f-4e2191206657",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `findOne` function fails for any reason (e.g., the database is down, there's a network error, etc.), the error will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have error handling in place for promises. This can be done using `.catch()` blocks or by using `try/catch` with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'id': uid\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (user) {\n        done(null, user);\n    } else {\n        done(null, false)\n    }\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle the error here\n    console.error(error);\n    done(error);\n});\n```\n\nIn this updated code, if `findOne` fails, the error will be caught and logged to the console, and the `done` callback will be called with the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Sequelize, a promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server. Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- sequelize\n- A database driver compatible with Sequelize (e.g., pg for Postgres, mysql2 for MySQL, etc.)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "42b8f573-9d55-357e-bc7a-e80238edf36b",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unspecified Path in Default Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when the path attribute in the session middleware settings is not explicitly defined. This can lead to potential security risks as the session cookie can be sent to unintended or malicious endpoints.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to explicitly set the path attribute in the session middleware settings. This ensures that the session cookie is only sent to the specified path, reducing the risk of it being intercepted by unintended or malicious endpoints.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { \n    secure: false,\n    path: '/' // specify the path\n  }\n}));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js\n- Express-session\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4302f234-74cb-32b5-97e5-811e920d6807",
              "name": "XML External Entity (XXE) Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `noent` attribute in the `libxmljs` library in JavaScript is used to substitute entities. When it is set to `true`, it can lead to XML External Entity (XXE) vulnerabilities. XXE vulnerabilities occur when an application processing XML input allows the inclusion of dynamic entities from external sources. This can lead to various security issues such as disclosure of internal files, denial of service, server-side request forgery, port scanning from the perspective of the machine where the parser is located, and other system impacts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to disable the loading of external entities in the XML parser. This can be done by setting the `noent` attribute to `false`. This will prevent the parser from loading external entities, thus mitigating the XXE vulnerability.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvar products = libxmljs.parseXmlString(req.files.products.data.toString('utf8'), {noent:false,noblanks:true})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `libxmljs`: A library for parsing and serializing XML and HTML to and from JavaScript objects.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XXE Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_External_Entity_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference ('XXE')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4755beb1-3945-31bf-9b76-0f3ddfb6293b",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnverified User Input IDOR (Insecure Direct Object References) Vulnerability is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a function with no proper access control is used to access specific resources directly by modifying the value of a parameter used in a direct object reference. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when the application uses user-supplied input to retrieve a database record without verifying if the user is authorized to access that record.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using the user-supplied login name (`req.body.login`) to find a user in the database. If an attacker can guess or brute-force the login name of another user, they can potentially access or modify that user's data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement proper access control checks to ensure that the user is authorized to access the requested resource. You should also validate and sanitize all user inputs to prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming `currentUser` is the logged-in user\nif (req.body.login === currentUser.login) {\n  db.User.find({\n    where: {\n      'login': req.body.login\n    }\n  }).then(user => {\n    // ...\n  });\n} else {\n  // Handle unauthorized access attempt\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we're checking if the login name in the request matches the login name of the currently logged-in user. If they don't match, we handle it as an unauthorized access attempt.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js for handling HTTP requests (`req.body.login`).\n- Sequelize.js for querying the database (`db.User.find`).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48b8013d-d557-3332-9145-6f6042113cc3",
              "name": "Admin Route Lacks Authorization Check",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Missing Authorization Check in Admin Route\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application does not properly protect sensitive functionality. This can allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to privileged functions, such as administrative functions. In the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the route `/admin/users` is not properly protected by an authorization check.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always perform authorization checks on sensitive routes. This can be done by implementing middleware functions that verify the user's role before allowing them to access certain routes. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `authHandler.isAuthenticated` middleware function is used to authenticate users. However, authentication is not enough for sensitive routes like `/admin/users`. You should also check if the authenticated user has the necessary privileges to access this route. \n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nrouter.get('/admin/users', [authHandler.isAuthenticated, authHandler.isAdmin], function(req, res){\n    res.render('app/adminusers')\n})\n```\n\nIn this version, `authHandler.isAdmin` is a middleware function that checks if the authenticated user is an admin. This function should return a 403 status code if the user is not an admin.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the Express.js library for routing and the `authHandler` middleware for authentication. Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- express\n- authHandler (this seems to be a custom library, so the actual name might be different)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4ba9ffd4-57a1-3ef8-b205-fb18267e4011",
              "name": "Command Injection Vulnerability In Shell Command",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand Injection Vulnerability through User Input Concatenation is a serious security flaw that can occur in JavaScript applications. This vulnerability arises when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data (forms, cookies, HTTP headers, etc.) to a system shell. In this case, the attacker can execute arbitrary commands on the host operating system using a maliciously crafted input.\n\nThe provided code snippet is vulnerable to command injection because it concatenates a user-supplied value (`req.body.address`) directly into a command that is executed by the system shell.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input directly in system commands. If it's necessary to include user input in system commands, the input should be properly sanitized and validated. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using the `child_process.execFile` function, which does not allow shell parameter expansion:\n\n```javascript\nvar execFile = require('child_process').execFile;\n\nexecFile('ping', ['-c', '2', req.body.address], function(err, stdout, stderr) {\n    output = stdout + stderr;\n    res.render('app/ping', {\n        output: output\n    });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js (for handling HTTP requests and responses)\n- child_process (Node.js built-in module for executing system commands)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e20ab92-5416-3d56-b05e-d175dadeae7f",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA Potential Path Traversal Vulnerability, also known as Directory Traversal, is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application. This is done by manipulating variables that reference files with \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user-supplied input is not properly sanitized and is used to construct a file path for file operations.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sequelize.import()` function is used to import a file. If the `file` variable is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it could lead to a path traversal vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid using user-supplied input to construct file paths directly.\n2. Use a whitelist of approved inputs.\n3. If user input must be used, ensure it is properly sanitized and validated. For example, disallow input containing \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations.\n4. Use built-in functions to construct file paths, such as `path.resolve()` and `path.normalize()` in Node.js.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar path = require('path');\nvar file = /* user-supplied input */;\nfile = path.basename(file);\nvar model = sequelize.import(path.join(__dirname, file));\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `path.basename()` function is used to get the last part of a path. This effectively prevents any \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences from traversing directories.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Path: A core Node.js module for handling and transforming file paths.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e889097-a1d1-341d-a4ec-b022bf36ab6d",
              "name": "Unescaped EJS Output Allows XSS Attacks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nEJS (Embedded JavaScript) is a popular templating engine for JavaScript. It allows developers to generate HTML with plain JavaScript. However, it can be vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if not used properly. Specifically, the `<%- include(vuln_reference) %>` syntax in EJS outputs unescaped HTML. This means that if `vuln_reference` contains malicious script, it will be executed in the user's browser when the page is loaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent XSS attacks, always escape user input that is included in the output HTML. In EJS, you can use `<%= include(vuln_reference) %>` to output escaped HTML. This will ensure that any script in `vuln_reference` is not executed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `<%- include(vuln_reference) %>` with `<%= include(vuln_reference) %>`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ejs` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "503041b8-7c2c-3470-86ff-ec0070f4844b",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) protection. CSRF is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the POST route '/bulkproductslegacy' does not have any CSRF protection. This means that if an attacker can trick a user into clicking a link or loading a page, the attacker can make requests to this route with the user's credentials.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement CSRF tokens in your POST routes. A CSRF token is a unique, random value associated with a user's session. It is used to verify that the user is the one who initiated a request.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can add CSRF protection to the given route using the `csurf` middleware:\n\n```javascript\nvar csrf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/bulkproductslegacy', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.bulkProductsLegacy);\n```\n\nIn this code, `csrfProtection` is a middleware that validates CSRF tokens. If the token is missing or invalid, it will throw an error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `csurf`: CSRF protection middleware for Express.\n\nYou can install these libraries using npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express csurf\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "58198161-e516-3982-8232-399ce77639fa",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand Injection is a type of vulnerability where an attacker is able to execute arbitrary commands on the host operating system via a vulnerable application. This vulnerability risk occurs when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data (forms, cookies, HTTP headers, etc.) to a system shell. In this case, the attacker-supplied operating system commands are usually executed with the privileges of the vulnerable application.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is vulnerable to command injection because it directly uses user input (`req.body.address`) in a system command (`ping -c 2 ' + req.body.address`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input directly in system commands. If it's necessary to include user input in system commands, the input should be properly sanitized and validated. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvar address = req.body.address;\nif (validator.isIP(address)) {\n    exec('ping -c 2 ' + address, function (err, stdout, stderr) {\n        // ...\n    });\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid IP address\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, the `validator.isIP()` function from the `validator` library is used to check if the user input is a valid IP address before it's used in the system command.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `child_process`: This is a built-in Node.js module used to execute system commands.\n- `express`: This is a web application framework for Node.js used to handle HTTP requests.\n- `validator`: This is a library of string validators and sanitizers.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-77: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command ('Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/77.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-77",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/77.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-77"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "58f37fb3-5791-3a3e-9b6f-e121432dfd65",
              "name": "Predictable Token Using Weak Hashing",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a security flaw where an attacker can manipulate the input to the MD5 hash function, potentially leading to unauthorized access or other security breaches. MD5 is a widely used cryptographic hash function that produces a 128-bit (16-byte) hash value. It is commonly used to verify data integrity. However, MD5 is not collision-resistant; as such, it is possible to find different inputs that hash to the same output, which is a vulnerability.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the application is comparing a token from the request body to an MD5 hash of a login from the request body. If an attacker can predict or control the `login` value, they might be able to craft a `token` that matches the MD5 hash, leading to a potential security breach.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following recommendations:\n\n1. **Avoid using MD5 for security-sensitive operations**: MD5 is considered broken in terms of collision resistance. For any security-sensitive operations, consider using stronger hash functions like SHA-256.\n\n2. **Use a Salt with your Hash**: A salt is a random data that is used as an additional input to a one-way function that hashes data, a password or passphrase. Salts are used to safeguard passwords in storage.\n\n3. **Use existing secure libraries for authentication**: Instead of implementing your own authentication mechanism, consider using existing libraries that have been thoroughly tested and vetted by the community.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the bcrypt library for hashing and checking the password:\n\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\n\n// Hash the password\nconst salt = bcrypt.genSaltSync(10);\nconst hashedPassword = bcrypt.hashSync(req.body.login, salt);\n\n// Store hashedPassword in the database\n\n// Check the password\nif (bcrypt.compareSync(req.body.token, hashedPassword)) {\n  // Passwords match\n} else {\n  // Passwords don't match\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- bcrypt: A library to help you hash passwords.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-328: Reversible One-Way Hash](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-328",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-328"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63477eba-0399-3c04-92f0-e6025397cb27",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return results directly to the client. An attacker can potentially gain access to data that they are not authorized to view.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `findAll` method that specifies the fields to be returned.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n  attributes: ['id', 'name', 'description'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(products => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `findAll` method is passed an object that specifies that only the 'id', 'name', and 'description' fields should be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "649ef06b-8197-3b90-b556-386f21d792c4",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Can Lead To Remote Code Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure Deserialization is a vulnerability that occurs when untrusted data is used to abuse the logic of an application, inflict a denial of service (DoS) attack, or execute arbitrary code upon it being deserialized. It also happens to be one of the OWASP Top 10 Application Security Risks.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is deserializing data from an untrusted source (`req.files.products.data.toString('utf8')`) without validating or sanitizing it first. This can lead to various security issues, including remote code execution, privilege escalation, and other serious data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid deserialization of untrusted data, if possible.\n2. Implement input validation. Do not rely on client-side validation only, as it can be easily bypassed.\n3. Use digital signatures to verify the source of serialized objects.\n4. Limit the classes of objects that can be deserialized.\n5. Log deserialization exceptions and failures, as they can indicate an attack.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can mitigate the vulnerability by validating the input before deserializing it. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nvar productsData = req.files.products.data.toString('utf8');\n\nif (isValid(productsData)) {\n    var products = serialize.unserialize(productsData);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid data\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `isValid` is a function that you would implement to validate the input data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `serialize` library for serialization and deserialization of data.\n- An HTTP library (like `express`) to handle the request (`req`).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A8:2017-Insecure Deserialization](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A8_2017-Insecure_Deserialization)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "64e8b5b0-0fc3-32d5-82cb-318cc0b99cc4",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In Unserialize Or Deserialize",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `unserialize()` or `deserialize()` function is used to convert a serialized string back into a JavaScript object. However, if the serialized string is controlled by user data, it can lead to a vulnerability known as Object Injection or Remote Code Injection. This vulnerability allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the server, potentially leading to a full system compromise.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `unserialize()` function is used to convert the `products` data from the request files into a JavaScript object. If an attacker can control the `products` data, they can inject malicious code that will be executed when the `unserialize()` function is called.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using the `unserialize()` or `deserialize()` function with user-controlled data. Instead, use a safe alternative such as JSON for data interchange. JSON.parse() is a safe alternative for `unserialize()` or `deserialize()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar products = JSON.parse(req.files.products.data.toString('utf8'));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not specify any library dependencies. However, it seems to be using the `serialize` library for the `unserialize()` function and the `express` library for handling HTTP requests.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A8-Insecure Deserialization](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A8_2017-Insecure_Deserialization)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65030232-f99c-3c71-9697-ef22772470db",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the database query fails for any reason (e.g., the database is down, there's a network error, etc.), the Promise rejection will not be handled, and the application may crash or behave unexpectedly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, which will catch any errors that occur in the preceding `.then()` blocks. In the `.catch()` block, you should handle the error appropriately, such as by logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you could modify the provided code to handle Promise rejections:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'login': req.query.login\n\t}\n}).then(user => {\n\tif (user) {\n\t\tif (req.query.token == md5(req.query.login)) {\n\t\t\tres.render('resetpw', {\n\t\t\t\tlogin: req.query.login,\n\t\t\t\ttoken: req.query.token\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\treq.flash('danger', \"Invalid reset token\")\n\t\t\tres.redirect('/forgotpw')\n\t\t}\n\t} else {\n\t\treq.flash('danger', \"Invalid login username\")\n\t\tres.redirect('/forgotpw')\n\t}\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A database library (such as Sequelize) for `db.User.find()`\n- Express.js for `req`, `res`, and `req.query`\n- A flash messages library (such as connect-flash) for `req.flash()`\n- The md5 library for `md5()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "69f8443d-6b76-3506-af0b-8b5795d5c1fe",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `db.User.findOne()` is not being caught if it gets rejected, which could lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a `.catch()` block when working with Promises to handle any potential errors. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, and prevents the application from crashing or behaving unexpectedly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the provided code with a `.catch()` block added to handle any potential errors:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'login': username\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (!user) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n    }\n    if (!isValidPassword(user, password)) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n    }\n    return done(null, user);\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle the error here\n    console.error(error);\n    return done(error);\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.findOne()`)\n- Express (for `req.flash()`)\n- Passport (for `done()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6afaab93-ad55-39a5-a7d4-66581a06b018",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the `User` database with a `login` field from the request body. If the `User` database contains sensitive fields, they could be returned in the query result and potentially exposed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done using the `attributes` option in the `find` method. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n    attributes: ['login'], // Only return the 'login' field\n    where: {\n        'login': req.body.login\n    }\n}).then(user => {\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `attributes` option is used to explicitly define that only the `login` field should be returned from the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "740c22eb-3230-38b4-830b-9c13894f68db",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but the rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `user.save()` promise is rejected, there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises. The `.catch()` block will be executed when the promise is rejected, allowing you to handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n    where: {\n        'login': req.body.login\n    }\n}).then(user => {\n    if (user) {\n        if (req.body.token == md5(req.body.login)) {\n            user.password = bCrypt.hashSync(req.body.password, bCrypt.genSaltSync(10), null)\n            user.save().then(function () {\n                req.flash('success', \"Password successfully reset\")\n                res.redirect('/login')\n            }).catch(err => {\n                console.error(err);\n                req.flash('danger', \"An error occurred while resetting password\")\n                res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n            })\n        } else {\n            req.flash('danger', \"Invalid reset token\")\n            res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n        }\n    } else {\n        req.flash('danger', \"Invalid login username\")\n        res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n    }\n}).catch(err => {\n    console.error(err);\n    req.flash('danger', \"An error occurred while finding user\")\n    res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.find()`)\n- Express.js (for `req`, `res`, `req.body`, `req.flash()`, `res.redirect()`)\n- bcryptjs (for `bCrypt.hashSync()`, `bCrypt.genSaltSync()`)\n- md5 (for `md5()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e8fb4c0-39b6-3c06-8ea5-e32e5a9fff36",
              "name": "Unescaped EJS Output Allows XSS Attacks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"EJS Output Unescaped Vulnerability Detected: Potential for XSS Attacks\" is a vulnerability in JavaScript programming language that occurs when user input is included in the output without proper sanitization or validation. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information like session cookies, or perform other harmful actions.\n\nEJS (Embedded JavaScript) is a templating engine used in JavaScript. The `<%- include(vuln_scenario) %>` syntax is used to include raw output in the template. If `vuln_scenario` contains user input, this can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user input that is included in the output. This can be done using the `<%= include(vuln_scenario) %>` syntax in EJS, which automatically escapes the output.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable code:\n\n```javascript\n<%- include(vuln_scenario) %>\n```\n\nwith the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\n<%= include(vuln_scenario) %>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ejs` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7eb7a67d-89f2-37e3-b65c-e4fa3be37804",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the `User` database, but no specific fields are selected, so all fields associated with the user will be returned, including potentially sensitive ones.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to include or exclude. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t},\n\tattributes: ['id', 'username', 'email'] // specify the fields to return\n}).then(user => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `attributes` option is used to specify the fields to be returned by the query. Only the `id`, `username`, and `email` fields will be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7edc0b19-6cd6-3a9d-a61b-f2e6b4ccd34e",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Can Lead To SQL Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. This is due to the fact that it directly concatenates user input (`req.body.login`) into a SQL query string. An attacker could potentially manipulate the `login` parameter to alter the SQL query, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this risk, it is recommended to use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can effectively separate SQL logic from data, thereby preventing an attacker from manipulating the SQL query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nvar query = \"SELECT name,id FROM Users WHERE login= :login\";\ndb.sequelize.query(query, {\n\tmodel: db.User,\n\treplacements: { login: req.body.login }\n}).then(user => {\n\t// rest of the code\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// error handling\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Express.js (for handling requests and responses)\n- Sequelize (for interacting with the database)\n- Connect-flash (for flash messages)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "83405d81-b933-3e5e-8590-b29d1042de24",
              "name": "Sql Injection Via User-Controlled Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPotential SQL Injection Due to User-Controlled Input in Raw SQL Query is a vulnerability that occurs when an application uses user-controlled data in a SQL query without properly sanitizing it. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the SQL query to perform unauthorized actions, such as retrieving, modifying, or deleting data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `query` variable is passed directly into the `db.sequelize.query()` function. If the `query` variable contains user-controlled data, this could lead to a SQL Injection vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using raw SQL queries whenever possible. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can ensure that user-controlled data is properly sanitized before it is used in a SQL query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.sequelize.query(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE id = :id\", \n{ \n    replacements: { id: req.body.id }, \n    type: db.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT,\n    model: db.User \n}).then(user => {\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `query` variable is replaced with a parameterized query. The `:id` placeholder in the query is replaced with the `id` value from the `req.body` object. This ensures that the `id` value is properly sanitized before it is used in the SQL query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "847db4e4-f722-3d7f-bdfb-23e1e52e4e98",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings Without Expiration Date",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is a configuration for session middleware in a Node.js application using Express.js. The vulnerability here is that the `expires` attribute in the cookie settings is not set. This means that the session cookie will not expire, which can lead to session hijacking or session sidejacking attacks if an attacker manages to steal the session cookie.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should set the `expires` attribute in the cookie settings to a reasonable value. This will ensure that the session cookie will expire after a certain period of time, reducing the window of opportunity for an attacker to steal the session cookie.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nvar session = require('express-session');\n\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { \n    secure: false,\n    expires: new Date(Date.now() + (60 * 1000 * 30)) // 30 minutes\n  }\n}));\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `expires` attribute is set to 30 minutes from the current time. This means that the session cookie will expire 30 minutes after it is set.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express\n- express-session\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-613: Insufficient Session Expiration](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/613.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-613",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/613.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-613"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8d0dc7b0-9014-3517-ac7c-42708d488023",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as SQL Injection, is a common security vulnerability that allows an attacker to manipulate SQL queries. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user input is directly used in a SQL query without proper sanitization or parameterization. In the provided code, the user input `req.body.name` is directly concatenated into a SQL `LIKE` clause, which could allow an attacker to manipulate the query.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, user input should never be directly used in a SQL query. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements. These techniques ensure that user input is always treated as literal data, not part of the SQL command.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code using Sequelize's built-in parameterization:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n\twhere: {\n\t\tname: {\n\t\t\t[Op.like]: Sequelize.literal(`%${req.body.name}%`)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(products => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following libraries:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "931995f3-23d7-34e1-b773-a7c4eaf32155",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have protection against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. In a CSRF attack, an attacker tricks a victim into performing actions on their behalf on a web application in which the victim is authenticated. \n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the POST route '/bulkproducts' does not have any CSRF protection. This means that if an authenticated user is tricked into clicking a malicious link or visiting a malicious website, the attacker can make POST requests to '/bulkproducts' on behalf of the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement CSRF protection on your POST routes. This can be done by using CSRF tokens. A CSRF token is a random, unique value associated with a user's session, which must be included in all form submissions. When a form is submitted, the server checks the CSRF token to ensure it matches the one stored in the user's session.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `csurf` middleware in Express.js to add CSRF protection to your routes. Here is how you can modify your code:\n\n```javascript\nvar csrf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/bulkproducts', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.bulkProducts);\n```\n\nIn this code, `csrfProtection` is a middleware function that validates the CSRF token in the request. If the token is missing or invalid, it sends a 403 Forbidden response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `csurf`: CSRF protection middleware for Express.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "933c0859-c78e-3c4b-ac8e-7ea875fcbbbc",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.User.findOne` and `db.User.create` are not handled properly. If these promises are rejected (for example, due to a database error), the rejection will not be caught and handled, leading to an unhandled promise rejection.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by attaching a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be called if the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should handle the error appropriately, for example by logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is the provided code with added error handling:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'email': username\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (user) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Account Already Exists'));\n    } else {\n        if (req.body.email && req.body.password && req.body.username && req.body.cpassword && req.body.name) {\n            if (req.body.cpassword == req.body.password) {\n                db.User.create({\n                    email: req.body.email,\n                    password: createHash(password),\n                    name: req.body.name,\n                    login: username\n                }).then(function (user) {\n                    return done(null, user)\n                }).catch(function (error) {\n                    // Handle error\n                    console.error(error);\n                    return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'An error occurred'));\n                });\n            } else {\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Passwords dont match'));\n            }\n        } else {\n            return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Input field(s) missing'));\n        }\n    }\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle error\n    console.error(error);\n    return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'An error occurred'));\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.findOne` and `db.User.create`)\n- Express (for `req` and `res`)\n- Connect-flash (for `req.flash`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "96bf9b6a-fcf1-3eab-be8e-47ba3cb8bd10",
              "name": "Insecure Logging Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization\" vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when the Sequelize ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) library is initialized without properly configuring the logging option. By default, Sequelize logs all SQL queries it executes. If these logs are not properly protected, they can expose sensitive information such as passwords, credit card numbers, or other personally identifiable information (PII). This can lead to serious security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable Sequelize's logging feature in production environments. If logging is necessary for debugging or other purposes, ensure that the logs are properly protected and that sensitive information is either not logged or is obfuscated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can disable logging by setting the `logging` option to `false` when initializing Sequelize:\n\n```javascript\nvar sequelize = new Sequelize(config.database, config.username, config.password, {\n    host: config.host,\n    dialect: config.dialect,\n    logging: false\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: This is the Sequelize ORM library.\n- `config`: This is not a library, but a configuration object that should contain the database connection details.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-532: Information Exposure Through Log Files](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-532",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-532"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "98a40837-bc37-3b75-a673-964885eb949c",
              "name": "User-Controlled ID Used For User Lookup Without Check",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnverified User ID Vulnerability in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when an application does not properly verify the user's identity before performing sensitive operations. In the provided code snippet, the application is fetching a user from the database based on the user ID received from the request body. If an attacker can manipulate the request body, they can potentially access other users' data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user inputs. Never trust data coming from the client side as it can be manipulated. Also, implement proper access control checks to ensure that a user can only access data that they are authorized to access.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nif (req.user.id !== req.body.id) {\n    // The logged in user's ID does not match the requested ID\n    res.status(403).send('Unauthorized');\n} else {\n    db.User.find({\n        where: {\n            'id': req.body.id\n        }\t\t\n    }).then(user => {\n        // Continue processing\n    });\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we are checking if the logged-in user's ID matches the ID in the request body. If they do not match, we return a 403 Unauthorized status.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js for handling HTTP requests and responses.\n- Sequelize for interacting with the database.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A5:2017-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9b0a2c63-a010-3479-a131-e071cb4a9bee",
              "name": "Directly Writing To Response Object May Cause XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a web application through an untrusted source, most frequently a web request, and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability is present when the application does not adequately validate, filter, or encode user-supplied input before including it in the generated HTML content.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript and Node.js, directly writing to the response object (e.g., `res.send(html)`) without properly sanitizing the `html` content can lead to XSS vulnerabilities. This is because an attacker can inject malicious scripts into the `html` content, which will then be executed by the browser when the HTML content is rendered.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and escape user-supplied input before including it in the generated HTML content. This can be done using various libraries such as `DOMPurify` or `xss`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `xss` library:\n\n```javascript\nvar xss = require('xss');\n\n// ...\n\nvar sanitizedHtml = xss(html);\nres.send(sanitizedHtml);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `xss` library. You can install it using npm:\n\n```\nnpm install xss\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d8c3327-ae28-32f7-877d-4c72615629b9",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnverified User Input IDOR (Insecure Direct Object References) Vulnerability is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a function with no access control is used to process user input. This can allow an attacker to bypass authorization and directly access resources in the system. In the given JavaScript code, the vulnerability exists because the `login` parameter in the `find` function is directly using user input from `req.body.login` without any validation or sanitization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input blindly.\n2. Implement proper access control mechanisms to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n3. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n4. Regularly update and patch your systems and libraries to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given code:\n\n```javascript\nconst Sequelize = require('sequelize');\nconst Op = Sequelize.Op;\n\ndb.User.find({\n  where: {\n    'login': {\n      [Op.eq]: req.body.login\n    }\n  }\n}).then(user => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, we are using Sequelize's `Op.eq` operator to ensure that the `login` value is exactly equal to `req.body.login`. This prevents the possibility of SQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d9c1240-eb9b-35fe-9ed3-d8eed633fa39",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unspecified Domain in Default Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that can occur when the domain attribute in the session cookie is not specified. This can lead to potential session hijacking or cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks as the session cookie can be sent to all subdomains of the parent domain, some of which may be malicious or vulnerable to attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to explicitly set the domain attribute in the session cookie settings. This restricts the session cookie to be sent only to the specified domain, reducing the risk of session hijacking or XSS attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar session = require('express-session');\nvar cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\n\nvar app = express();\napp.use(cookieParser());\n\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { \n    secure: false,\n    domain: 'yourdomain.com' // specify your domain here\n  }\n}));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express\n- express-session\n- cookie-parser\n\nYou can install these dependencies using npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express express-session cookie-parser\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-346: Origin Validation Error](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-346",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-346"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a4e17d51-43bf-3f5e-8dc7-8cd4e9209183",
              "name": "Open Redirect Vulnerability With User-Controlled Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUser-Controlled Input Redirect Vulnerability, also known as Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards, is a type of vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted data to determine the destination page. This vulnerability can lead to several types of attacks such as phishing attacks, malicious redirects, or forcing the user to perform an action without their consent.\n\nIn the given code snippet `res.redirect(req.query.url)`, the application is redirecting the user to a URL provided in the request query. If an attacker can control the `url` parameter, they can redirect users to malicious websites.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input to determine the destination of redirects without proper validation and sanitization. If it's necessary to redirect based on user input, ensure that the input is validated against a whitelist of allowed destinations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/redirect', function(req, res) {\n    var url = req.query.url;\n    var allowedUrls = ['http://example.com', 'http://example.org'];\n\n    if (allowedUrls.indexOf(url) !== -1) {\n        res.redirect(url);\n    } else {\n        res.redirect('/error');\n    }\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, we are only allowing redirects to URLs that are in the `allowedUrls` array. If the provided URL is not in the array, we redirect to an error page.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Express.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7c0102e-bb5c-38ad-94d0-e0ae27ec572c",
              "name": "Unescaped EJS Output Allows XSS Attacks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Unescaped EJS Output Vulnerability Allowing XSS Attacks\" vulnerability refers to a security flaw in JavaScript programming where the output is not properly escaped. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. In this case, the vulnerability is in the EJS (Embedded JavaScript) templates. EJS is a simple templating language that lets you generate HTML markup with plain JavaScript. If the output is not properly escaped, it can lead to XSS attacks where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always escape output that is dynamically included in HTML. This can be done using the `<%= %>` syntax in EJS, which automatically escapes the output. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink can be fixed by changing the `<%- %>` syntax to `<%= %>`. The fixed code would look like this:\n\n```javascript\nListing products with <strong>search query: </strong> <%= output.searchTerm %>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- EJS\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ae5be0ea-85a4-3b69-8e33-cdbd99d871ce",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including potential crashes or unhandled exceptions. In the context of Node.js, an unhandled promise rejection can cause the Node.js process to terminate.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.find()` is not being handled for potential rejection. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem connecting to the database), the rejection will not be caught and the application may crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises, which will catch any errors that occur during the execution of the promise.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.query.id\n\t}\n}).then(product => {\n\tif (!product) {\n\t\tproduct = {}\n\t}\n\toutput = {\n\t\tproduct: product\n\t}\n\tres.render('app/modifyproduct', {\n\t\toutput: output\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle the error here\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, if the promise is rejected, the `.catch()` block will be executed, logging the error and sending a 500 response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.Product.find()`)\n- Express (for `req.query.id` and `res.render()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "af85e538-7a8c-38df-928d-6f0e290f5ae1",
              "name": "Exposed Sensitive Data In API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an API unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when a developer includes sensitive data in the response object without realizing it, or when the API is not properly configured to filter out sensitive data. In the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability arises from the fact that all user data is being returned in the API response without any filtering or sanitization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate, sanitize, and filter API responses to ensure that no sensitive data is included unintentionally.\n2. Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n3. Use encryption to protect sensitive data in transit and at rest.\n4. Regularly review and update your API's configuration settings to ensure that they are secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can mitigate the vulnerability by filtering out sensitive data from the `users` object before sending it in the response. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findAll({}).then(users => {\n    const safeUsers = users.map(user => {\n        const { password, ...safeUser } = user;\n        return safeUser;\n    });\n\n    res.status(200).json({\n        success: true,\n        users: safeUsers\n    });\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, we're using the `map` function to create a new array of user objects that don't include the `password` property.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security Top 10 2019](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b1654fa1-d62e-325d-ace5-658ec6a29678",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. In this case, the user-controlled data (`req.body.address`) is directly used in an `exec` function, which can lead to command injection if the user input is not properly sanitized. Moreover, the output of the command is directly rendered in the HTML without any sanitization, which can lead to XSS attacks if the command output contains any HTML tags.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using `exec` with user-controlled data. If you must use it, make sure to sanitize the user input properly.\n2. Always sanitize any data that is going to be rendered in the HTML. This can be done using various libraries that provide HTML encoding functions.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a possible fix using the `child_process` library's `execFile` function, which does not allow command injection, and the `escape-html` library to sanitize the output:\n\n```javascript\nvar escape = require('escape-html');\nvar execFile = require('child_process').execFile;\n\nexecFile('ping', ['-c', '2', req.body.address], function (err, stdout, stderr) {\n    output = escape(stdout + stderr);\n    res.render('app/ping', {\n        output: output\n    });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: for handling HTTP requests and responses.\n- `child_process`: for executing shell commands.\n- `escape-html`: for sanitizing the output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Command Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b3887378-67c6-3dc5-96db-c29c32eca81b",
              "name": "Xxe Vulnerability In Xml Parsing Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe XXE (XML External Entity) vulnerability in libxmljs configured with `noent:true` is a security flaw in JavaScript programming language. This vulnerability allows an attacker to exploit the XML parser by injecting malicious XML content. When the parser processes the XML content, it can lead to disclosure of confidential data, denial of service, server side request forgery, port scanning from the perspective of the machine where the parser is located, and other system impacts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable the use of external entities in your XML parser. This can be done by setting the `noent` option to `false` when parsing XML strings. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the specific vulnerability sink:\n\n```javascript\nvar products = libxmljs.parseXmlString(req.files.products.data.toString('utf8'), {noent:false,noblanks:true})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- libxmljs\n- express (for handling requests, assuming `req` is an Express.js request object)\n- multer (for handling file uploads, assuming `req.files.products` is a file uploaded via Multer)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XXE Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_External_Entity_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference ('XXE')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ba65a966-dca8-3468-92f7-8ff26d961d83",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.User.find()` and `user.save()` are not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using async/await with try/catch blocks. This ensures that any errors that occur during the execution of the promise are properly caught and handled, preventing unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t}\t\t\n}).then(user =>{\n\tif(req.body.password.length>0){\n\t\tif(req.body.password.length>0){\n\t\t\tif (req.body.password == req.body.cpassword) {\n\t\t\t\tuser.password = bCrypt.hashSync(req.body.password, bCrypt.genSaltSync(10), null)\n\t\t\t}else{\n\t\t\t\treq.flash('warning', 'Passwords dont match')\n\t\t\t\tres.render('app/useredit', {\n\t\t\t\t\tuserId: req.user.id,\n\t\t\t\t\tuserEmail: req.user.email,\n\t\t\t\t\tuserName: req.user.name,\n\t\t\t\t})\n\t\t\t\treturn\t\t\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}else{\n\t\t\treq.flash('warning', 'Invalid Password')\n\t\t\tres.render('app/useredit', {\n\t\t\t\tuserId: req.user.id,\n\t\t\t\tuserEmail: req.user.email,\n\t\t\t\tuserName: req.user.name,\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tuser.email = req.body.email\n\tuser.name = req.body.name\n\tuser.save().then(function () {\n\t\treq.flash('success',\"Updated successfully\")\n\t\tres.render('app/useredit', {\n\t\t\tuserId: req.body.id,\n\t\t\tuserEmail: req.body.email,\n\t\t\tuserName: req.body.name,\n\t\t})\n\t}).catch(err => {\n\t\t// Handle error here\n\t\tconsole.error(err);\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle error here\n\tconsole.error(err);\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.find()` and `user.save()`)\n- bcrypt (for `bCrypt.hashSync()`)\n- Express (for `req` and `res`)\n- Connect-flash (for `req.flash()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
