            {
              "id": "bb67020f-fbd7-3374-a825-f2e1db570826",
              "name": "Assigning To InnerHTML Can Introduce DOM XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential DOM XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is assigned to `innerHTML` in JavaScript. This is because `innerHTML` interprets any HTML that it is given, which includes potentially malicious scripts. If an attacker can control the input that is assigned to `innerHTML`, they can inject a script that will be executed by the browser.\n\nIn the provided code example, `users[i].name` is assigned to `c_name.innerHTML`. If `users[i].name` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, this could lead to a DOM XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid assigning user-controlled data to `innerHTML`. Instead, consider using `textContent` or `innerText`, which do not interpret HTML and therefore are not vulnerable to script injection.\n\nIf you must use `innerHTML`, ensure that the user-controlled data is properly sanitized before it is assigned. This can be done by using a library that provides HTML sanitization functions, or by implementing your own sanitization function that removes or escapes any potentially dangerous characters or sequences.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code example:\n\n```javascript\nc_name.textContent = users[i].name;\n```\n\nIn this version, `textContent` is used instead of `innerHTML`, so any HTML in `users[i].name` will be treated as plain text and not interpreted by the browser.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c04545fa-e5e3-37be-8c6f-6c464b21fae9",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure Direct Object References (IDOR) vulnerability occurs when an application provides direct access to objects based on user-supplied input. In this case, the application uses user-supplied input without proper validation or authorization checks. This can allow an attacker to manipulate these references to access unauthorized data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is directly using the user-supplied input (`req.body.id`) to query the database for a product. If an attacker manipulates the `id` parameter in the request, they could potentially access data they are not authorized to view.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, the application should perform proper input validation and authorization checks before using user-supplied input to access data. \n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a simple example of how you could validate and authorize the user input before using it to query the database:\n\n```javascript\nif (req.user && req.user.id === req.body.id) {\n\tdb.Product.find({\n\t\twhere: {\n\t\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t\t}\n\t}).then(product => {\n\t\t// ...\n\t});\n} else {\n\t// Handle unauthorized access attempt...\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the application checks if the user is authenticated and if the authenticated user's ID matches the ID provided in the request. If not, the application rejects the request.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js (for handling HTTP requests)\n- Sequelize (for querying the database)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c183bb47-9c87-3d38-bf40-65eaba0f7c7b",
              "name": "Failed Login Attempt Without Explicit Logging",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unlogged Failed Authentication Attempt Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the situation where failed login attempts are not logged by the system. This can be a security risk as it allows attackers to attempt to gain unauthorized access to the system without being detected. In the provided code, the system does not log failed login attempts, which means that an attacker could repeatedly try to log in with different credentials without raising any alarms.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to implement logging for failed login attempts. This can help to identify and block potential brute force attacks. Additionally, consider implementing account lockouts or delays after a certain number of failed login attempts to further deter attackers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to add logging to the provided code:\n\n```javascript\npassport.use('login', new LocalStrategy({\n        passReqToCallback: true\n    },\n    function (req, username, password, done) {\n        db.User.findOne({\n            where: {\n                'login': username\n            }\n        }).then(function (user) {\n            if (!user) {\n                console.log('Failed login attempt for username: ' + username); // Log failed login attempt\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n            }\n            if (!isValidPassword(user, password)) {\n                console.log('Failed login attempt for username: ' + username); // Log failed login attempt\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n            }\n            return done(null, user);\n        });\n    }));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `passport`\n- `passport-local`\n- `express-flash`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-287: Improper Authentication](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-287",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c19db147-b975-32c4-8756-28106ee3991d",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) protection. CSRF is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\nIn the given JavaScript code, the POST route '/useredit' lacks CSRF protection. This means that if an attacker can trick a user into clicking a link or loading a page, the attacker can make requests to this route with the user's cookies, potentially leading to unauthorized actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use CSRF tokens. A CSRF token is a unique, random value associated with a user's session. It is used to verify that the user is the one who initiated a request. \n\nIn Express.js, you can use the `csurf` middleware to add CSRF protection to your routes. \n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nFirst, install the `csurf` middleware:\n\n```bash\nnpm install csurf\n```\n\nThen, use it in your application:\n\n```javascript\nvar csrf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/useredit', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.userEditSubmit);\n```\n\nThis will add CSRF protection to the '/useredit' route. The `csurf` middleware will add a `_csrf` property to the `req.body` object. You should include this value as a hidden field in your forms.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- express\n- csurf\n- Any libraries required by `authHandler` and `appHandler`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c6f882aa-7e66-30be-810b-64a4fa9750ea",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as NoSQL Injection, is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application doesn't properly validate or escape input before using it in a database query. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the query, potentially leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the `login` parameter from the request query is directly used in the database query without any validation or sanitization. This means that an attacker could potentially manipulate the `login` parameter to alter the database query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in a database query. This can be done using various methods, such as using prepared statements, parameterized queries, or ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) libraries that automatically handle these issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you could fix the provided code using Sequelize, an ORM library for Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst { Op } = require('sequelize');\n\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'login': {\n\t\t\t[Op.eq]: req.query.login\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(user => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, we're using Sequelize's `Op.eq` operator to ensure that the `login` parameter is used as a direct equality comparison in the database query, preventing any potential manipulation of the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the Sequelize ORM library for Node.js. Therefore, the `sequelize` package would be required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP: Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet in Java](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet_in_Java.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cf351ee9-72c0-3a39-a678-efd0b8996fd3",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. For instance, if a user object in the database contains a password field, and a query is made to fetch all users without specifying the fields to be returned, the password field will be included in the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `attributes` option in the query, where the keys are the field names and the values are true if the field should be returned and false otherwise.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findAll({\n  attributes: ['id', 'username', 'email'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(users => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, only the 'id', 'username', and 'email' fields will be returned in the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc91c059-b8d7-3ca2-81bb-4e6950613c8c",
              "name": "SQL Injection Due To Unsanitized User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Potential SQL Injection Detected in Sequelize Statement due to Unsanitized User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses unsanitized user input in a Sequelize query. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the SQL query and perform unauthorized actions on the database, such as viewing, modifying, or deleting data. This is a type of SQL Injection attack, which is a common and serious security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user input before using it in a SQL query. This can be done by using parameterized queries or prepared statements, which ensure that user input is always treated as data and not as part of the SQL command.\n\nIn addition, you should also validate user input on the server side to ensure it meets the expected format and does not contain any malicious characters or sequences. This can be done using regular expressions or a validation library.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use a parameterized query with Sequelize to prevent SQL Injection:\n\n```javascript\nlet userId = req.params.id; // user input\n\ndb.sequelize.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = :userId', \n  { \n    replacements: { userId: userId }, \n    type: db.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT \n  })\n  .then(users => {\n    // ...\n  });\n```\n\nIn this example, `:userId` is a placeholder that Sequelize will replace with the sanitized `userId` value.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "de1f49a6-2920-3aef-8b5c-f910e778fc83",
              "name": "Explicit Unescape In EJS Template",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"EJS Template XSS Vulnerability Detected Due to Unescaped External Data\" is a vulnerability that occurs when user-supplied data is not properly sanitized or escaped before being rendered in an EJS template. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information, perform actions on behalf of the user, or exploit other vulnerabilities in the user's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always escape user-supplied data before rendering it in an EJS template. This can be done using the `<%- %>` syntax in EJS, which automatically escapes HTML characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `searchTerm` is not being escaped before being rendered. This can be fixed by changing the `<%- %>` syntax to `<%= %>`, which automatically escapes HTML characters.\n\n```javascript\nListing products with <strong>search query: </strong> <%= output.searchTerm %>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- EJS\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e03af7d2-f1de-3621-833f-d754941defe6",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code, the application is taking user input directly through `req.params.vuln` and including it in the HTML response without any sanitization or encoding. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, leading to a potential XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Never insert untrusted data except in allowed locations.\n- HTML escape before inserting untrusted data into HTML element content.\n- Attribute escape before inserting untrusted data into HTML common attributes.\n- JavaScript escape before inserting untrusted data into JavaScript data values.\n- Always validate user input for length, characters, format, and business rules before accepting the data to be displayed or stored.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nThe Express.js framework provides a way to escape HTML through the use of template engines like EJS or Pug. Here is an example of how you can escape HTML using EJS:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('vulnerabilities/layout', {\n\tvuln: req.params.vuln,\n\tvuln_title: vulnDict[req.params.vuln],\n\tvuln_scenario: req.params.vuln + '/scenario',\n\tvuln_description: req.params.vuln + '/description',\n\tvuln_reference: req.params.vuln + '/reference',\n\tvulnerabilities:vulnDict\n}, function (err, html) {\n\tif (err) {\n\t\tconsole.log(err)\n\t\tres.status(404).send('404')\n\t} else {\n\t\tres.send(html)\n\t}\n})\n```\n\nIn your EJS template, you would escape the output like this:\n\n```ejs\n<%= vuln %>\n```\n\nThe `<%=` tags will HTML escape the output.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following dependencies:\n\n- Express.js\n- EJS or another template engine\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e4109124-a7e3-3004-8776-aa4f6b683e25",
              "name": "Weak Cryptographic Hash Algorithm Used",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure Cryptographic Hash Identified: MD5, SHA1 Inadequate for Security; bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 Recommended for Passwords, SHA-256+ for Integrity Checks is a vulnerability that arises when cryptographic hash functions with known weaknesses, such as MD5 and SHA1, are used in security-critical contexts. These hash functions are considered insecure due to their susceptibility to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to unauthorized access or data integrity issues.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using libraries such as 'md5' to hash sensitive data like passwords. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger cryptographic hash functions. For password hashing, bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 are recommended due to their resistance to brute-force and rainbow table attacks. For integrity checks, SHA-256 or higher is recommended.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the 'md5' library with a more secure hashing library. Here is an example using 'bcrypt':\n\n```javascript\nvar bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\nvar saltRounds = 10;\n\nbcrypt.hash('myPassword', saltRounds, function(err, hash) {\n  // Store hash in your password DB.\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe 'bcrypt' library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e9af2c2f-796a-31f5-b7d1-e8128751ed05",
              "name": "Deprecated Mathjs Eval Vulnerable To Code Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `mathjs.eval()` function in the Math.js library is deprecated due to its susceptibility to code injection attacks. This function evaluates a string as a mathematical expression within the JavaScript environment, which can lead to arbitrary code execution if a malicious user can control the input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using the `mathjs.eval()` function and instead use the `mathjs.evaluate()` function, which is safer and not susceptible to code injection attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the deprecated `mathjs.eval()` function with the `mathjs.evaluate()` function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\n// Vulnerable code\noutput: mathjs.eval(req.body.eqn)\n\n// Fixed code\noutput: mathjs.evaluate(req.body.eqn)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed4592b8-8077-3651-939b-39c3fa4ff342",
              "name": "Insecure Default Session Cookie Name",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Default Session Cookie Name Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the use of default or predictable session cookie names in web applications. This can potentially expose the application to session fixation attacks, where an attacker can predict the session cookie name and hijack the user session.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the session middleware is used with its default settings, which includes a default session cookie name. This can make the application vulnerable to session fixation attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a custom, unpredictable session cookie name. This can be done by setting the `name` option in the session middleware configuration.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst session = require('express-session');\n\napp.use(session({\n  name: 'sessionID', // use a custom, unpredictable session cookie name\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { secure: false }\n}));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `express-session`: A middleware for handling session in Express applications.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-384: Session Fixation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/384.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-384",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/384.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-384"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f0fdd933-a28a-3c62-96c9-3ab6fdb8e3bc",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings For Session Management",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\" in JavaScript, specifically in Node.js applications using Express.js, refers to the insecure configuration of session middleware. In the provided code, the session cookie is not set to be secure. This means that the cookie can be transmitted over an insecure HTTP connection, making it vulnerable to interception and potential misuse.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should set the `secure` option to `true` in the session middleware settings. This ensures that the session cookie is only sent over HTTPS, which is a secure protocol. However, this requires that your server is set up to use HTTPS.\n\nIn addition, you should consider using the `httpOnly` option to prevent the cookie from being accessed through client-side scripts, which can help to mitigate cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. You should also use the `sameSite` option to mitigate cross-site request forgery (CSRF) attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { \n    secure: true,\n    httpOnly: true,\n    sameSite: true\n  }\n}))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `express-session`: A middleware for handling session in Express.js applications.\n\nYou can install these dependencies using npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express express-session\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-614: Sensitive Cookie in HTTPS Session Without 'Secure' Attribute](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/614.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-614",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/614.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-614"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f515b20b-35a1-3eca-92a1-5a132b0ced80",
              "name": "Dynamic Execution Of User Input Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `mathjs.eval` function in the Math.js library is a powerful tool that can evaluate mathematical expressions. However, it can also evaluate arbitrary JavaScript code, which makes it a potential security risk. If user input is passed directly to this function without proper sanitization, it can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE) vulnerabilities. This is known as the \"Mathjs.eval Request Data Vulnerability\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `mathjs.eval` with user-supplied input. If you need to evaluate mathematical expressions from user input, consider using a safer alternative that doesn't allow arbitrary JavaScript execution.\n\nIf you must use `mathjs.eval`, make sure to sanitize and validate the user input before passing it to the function. This can help prevent malicious JavaScript code from being executed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `mathjs.eval` directly with user input, you can use `mathjs.parse` and `mathjs.compile` to safely evaluate mathematical expressions. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nconst mathjs = require('mathjs');\n\nlet userExpression = req.body.eqn;\nlet scope = {};\n\ntry {\n    let node = mathjs.parse(userExpression);\n    let code = node.compile();\n    let result = code.evaluate(scope);\n    output = result;\n} catch (err) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `mathjs.parse` creates a syntax tree from the user input, and `mathjs.compile` creates a callable function from the syntax tree. This function can then be safely evaluated with `code.evaluate`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- Math.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fbf64122-690b-3c9a-8a6a-23a189524adc",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to retrieve a user from the database based on the `login` field. However, all fields associated with the user are returned, which may include sensitive data such as passwords or personal information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should explicitly define the fields to be returned from the database query. This can be done using the `attributes` option in the `find` method. By specifying only the necessary fields, developers can ensure that sensitive data is not inadvertently exposed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n    where: {\n        'login': req.body.login\n    },\n    attributes: ['login', 'name', 'email'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(user => {\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, only the `login`, `name`, and `email` fields are returned from the database query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fca7bab8-6ef9-3e6b-8613-c6bdf1ebf8c2",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.findAll()` is not being handled for potential rejections.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using `try/catch` with async/await. This allows you to handle errors gracefully and prevent unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll()\n  .then(products => {\n    output = {\n      products: products\n    }\n    res.render('app/products', {\n      output: output\n    })\n  })\n  .catch(err => {\n    // Handle the error here\n    console.error(err);\n    res.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, a `.catch()` block is added to handle any errors that may occur when fetching products from the database.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Sequelize (for `db.Product.findAll()`) and Express (for `res.render()`). Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- Sequelize\n- Express\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "isComprehensive": false
        }
